#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:4
msgid "Audio effects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:6
msgid "Godot includes several audio effects that can be added to an audio bus to alter every sound that goes through that bus."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:11
msgid "Understanding how each effect works can be difficult, so don't feel discouraged if you have to look things up! If you are new to audio, an understanding of the essential effects can help you in most cases! Those are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:15
msgid "Equalizer & Filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:16
#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:222
msgid "Limiter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:17
msgid "Delay & Reverb"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:19
msgid "Try every effect out to get a sense of how they alter sound."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:23
msgid ":ref:`AudioSample <class_AudioSample>` does not support these effects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:25
msgid "Here follows short descriptions of the available effects:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:28
msgid "Amplify"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:30
msgid "Changes the volume of the sound. Some care needs to be taken, though: setting the volume level too high can digitally clip the sound, which can produce unpleasant crackles and pops. Consider using a :ref:`hard limiter <doc_hard_limiter>` or a :ref:`compressor <doc_compressor>` to prevent clipping, or :ref:`distortion <doc_distortion>` on clip mode if clipping at or below 0 dB is desired."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:40
msgid "BandLimitFilter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:42
msgid "A \"band-limit\" filter attenuates the frequencies at the *cutoff* point, and allows frequencies outside of that point to pass unchanged. It is similar to the :ref:`notch filter <doc_notch_filter>`, but weaker. It is the opposite of the :ref:`band-pass filter <doc_band_pass_filter>`. This filter can be used to give more room for other sounds to play at the cutoff point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:51
msgid "BandPassFilter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:53
msgid "A \"band-pass\" filter allow frequencies at the *cutoff* point to pass unchanged, and attenuates frequencies outside of that point. It is the opposite of the :ref:`band-limit filter <doc_band_limit_filter>` and :ref:`notch filter <doc_notch_filter>`. This filter can be used to simulate sounds passing through an old telephone line or megaphone. Modulating the cutoff point can simulate the sound of a wah-wah guitar pedal, think of the guitar in Jimi Hendrix's *Voodoo Child (Slight Return)*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:62
msgid "Capture"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:64
msgid "Copies the audio samples of the audio bus that this effect is attached to into an internal ring buffer. This can be used to capture data from the microphone or to transmit audio over the network in real-time. In general, it can be used to store real-time audio data for playback, and even to create real-time audio visualization, like an oscilloscope. This effect does not alter the audio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:72
msgid "Chorus"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:74
msgid "A \"chorus\" effect duplicates a signal and very slightly alters the timing and pitch of each duplicate, and modulates them over time via an LFO (low-frequency oscillator). The duplicates (also called \"voices\") are then mixed back together with the original signal, giving the impression that the sound comes from multiple sources. In the real world, this kind of effect is found in pianos, choirs, and instrument ensembles. This effect can also be used to widen mono audio and make digital sounds have a more natural or analog quality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:86
msgid "Compressor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:88
msgid "A \"compressor\" automatically attenuates (or \"ducks\") the volume of the input signal when its amplitude exceeds a certain volume threshold. The amount of attenuation applied is proportional to how far the input audio exceeds the threshold. The compressor's Ratio parameter controls the degree of attenuation. One of the main uses of a compressor is to reduce the dynamic range of signals with very loud and quiet parts. Reducing the dynamic range of a signal can make it fit more comfortably in a mix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:96
msgid "The compressor has many uses. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:98
msgid "It can be used in the Master bus to compress the whole output before it hits a limiter's ceiling, which makes the effect of the limiter much more subtle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:100
msgid "It can be used in voice clips to ensure they sound as even as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:101
msgid "It can be *sidechained* by another sound source. This means it can reduce the volume of one signal by using the volume of another audio bus for threshold detection. This technique is very common in video game mixing to \"duck\" the volume of music or sound effects when in-game or multiplayer voices need to be fully audible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:106
msgid "It can accentuate *transients* by using a slower attack, which lets louder parts go through before they are compressed. This can emphasize the \"punchiness\" of sound effects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:112
msgid "If your goal is solely to prevent a signal from exceeding a given amplitude altogether, a :ref:`hard limiter <doc_hard_limiter>` is likely a better choice than a compressor for this purpose. However, applying compression before a limiter is still good practice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:120
msgid "Delay"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:122
msgid "A \"delay\" effect duplicates a signal and repeats it multiple times, with a short period of time between each repetition (also called \"tap\"). Taps decay in volume over time. All of this creates an echo effect. Delay is great to simulate the acoustic space of a canyon or large room, where sound bounces off of surfaces and arrives at the listener after some *delay*. This is similar to :ref:`reverb <doc_reverb>`, which has a more natural and blurred sound to it. Using delay in conjunction with reverb can create very natural sounding environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:134
msgid "Distortion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:136
msgid "A \"distortion\" effect modifies the volume of the sound in a way that changes its waveform, which can result in a \"harsh\" and \"bright\" sound."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:139
msgid "Here are some of the distortion types that Godot offers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:141
msgid "*Clip*: clamps the volume of the sound, which makes it sound harsh."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:142
msgid "*Overdrive*: sounds like a guitar distortion pedal or megaphone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:143
msgid "*Lo-fi*: reduces the *bit depth* of the signal, emulating old speakers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:145
msgid "All types of distortion can add higher frequencies to the original sound, which helps it stand out better in a mix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:150
msgid "Be careful with the amount of distortion added, as it can create very harsh and loud sounds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:154
msgid "EQ"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:156
msgid "An \"equalizer\" gives you control over the gain of frequencies in the entire spectrum, through the use of \"bands\" which represent different regions of the spectrum. Equalizers can be essential to achieve a cleaner mix, allowing multiple sounds to play together without frequencies competing with each other. An equalizer on the Master bus can be useful to attenuate low and high frequencies that the device's speakers can't reproduce well. For example, phone and tablet speakers usually don't reproduce low frequency sounds well, and could make a limiter or compressor attenuate the Master volume more than necessary. This effect can be disabled when headphones are plugged in, which gives the user the best of both worlds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:169
msgid "This audio effect is what all other equalizers inherit from. It can be extended with custom scripts to create an equalizer with a custom number of bands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:174
msgid "EQ6, EQ10, EQ21"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:176
msgid "Godot provides three equalizers with different numbers of bands, which are represented in the title (6, 10, and 21 bands, respectively)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:180
msgid "Filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:182
msgid "A \"filter\" controls the gain of frequencies, through the use of a *cutoff* as a frequency threshold. It differs from an equalizer in that it uses different \"shapes\" to control frequencies; meaning frequencies will have their gain adjusted whether they are lower, higher, at, or outside the cutoff point, depending on the filter type. Filters can help give room to each sound and create interesting effects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:191
msgid "This audio effect is what all other filters inherit from. It should not be used directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:197
msgid "HardLimiter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:199
msgid "A \"limiter\" disallows audio signals from exceeding a given volume threshold level. Hard limiters predict volume peaks, and will smoothly apply gain reduction when the volume crosses the ceiling threshold level. It works similarly to a compressor, but is designed to not let the volume cross a certain volume level at all. Adding a limiter as the last effect of the Master bus is good practice, as it offers an easy safeguard against clipping. If clipping is desired, consider using the :ref:`distortion <doc_distortion>` effect on clip mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:209
msgid "HighPassFilter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:211
msgid "A \"high-pass\" filter attenuates frequencies lower than the *cutoff* point and allows higher frequencies to pass unchanged. This filter can be used to remove the bass content of a signal, making it sound \"thinner\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:216
msgid "HighShelfFilter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:218
msgid "A \"high-shelf\" filter controls the gain of all frequencies above the *cutoff* point. This filter can be used to increase or decrease clarity of a sound."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:226
msgid "This is the old limiter effect, and it is recommended to use the new :ref:`hard limiter <doc_hard_limiter>` effect instead. This effect is kept to preserve compatibility, however it should be considered deprecated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:230
msgid "Here is an example of how this effect works: if the ceiling is set to -12 dB, and the threshold is 0 dB, all samples going through get reduced by 12 dB. This changes the waveform of the sound and introduces distortion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:235
msgid "LowPassFilter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:237
msgid "A \"low-pass\" filter attenuates frequencies higher than the *cutoff* point and allows lower frequencies to pass unchanged. Low pass filters can be used to simulate \"muffled\" sounds. For instance, underwater sounds, sounds blocked by walls, or distant sounds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:243
msgid "LowShelfFilter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:245
msgid "A \"low-shelf\" filter controls the gain of all frequencies below the *cutoff* point. This filter can be used to adjust the \"strength\" of a sound, by increasing or decreasing the gain of the bass range."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:252
msgid "NotchFilter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:254
msgid "A \"notch\" filter attenuates the frequencies at the *cutoff* point, and allows frequencies outside of that point to pass unchanged. It is the opposite of the :ref:`band-pass filter <doc_band_pass_filter>`. This filter can be used to give more room for other sounds to play at the cutoff point. Because of how much it attenuates frequencies, it can also be used to completely remove very specific and undesired frequencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:262
msgid "Panner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:264
msgid "Moves the sound to the left or right. Headphones are recommended when configuring this effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:269
msgid "This effect may not be necessary with :ref:`AudioStreamPlayer2D <class_AudioStreamPlayer2D>` and :ref:`AudioStreamPlayer3D <class_AudioStreamPlayer3D>`, since they handle panning automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:275
msgid "Phaser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:277
msgid "A \"phaser\" effect creates a copy that is out-of-phase and mixes back together with the original. The copy is then modulated by an LFO (low-frequency oscillator), which makes some frequencies cancel each other out in interesting ways. The result of that is a series of peaks and troughs that sweep across the spectrum. This effect can be used to create sci-fi effects or Darth Vader-like voices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:285
msgid "PitchShift"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:287
msgid "Allows the adjustment of the signal's pitch independently of its speed. All frequencies can be raised or lowered with minimal effect on *transients*. This effect can be useful to create unusually high or deep voices. Do note that altering pitch can sound unnatural when pushed outside of a narrow window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:293
msgid "Record"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:295
msgid "Stores audio data into an :ref:`AudioStreamWAV <class_AudioStreamWAV>`. One usage example of this effect is to record microphone input and save as a WAV file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:302
msgid "Reverb"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:304
msgid "A \"reverb\" effect plays a copy of the input audio back continuously, which decays over a period of time, and creates a blurry echo effect (or \"reverberation\"). Reverb is great to simulate sounds in different kinds of spaces, which can range from small rooms to big caverns. This is similar to :ref:`delay <doc_delay>`, which has a less blurry sound to it. Using reverb in conjunction with delay can create very natural sounding environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:311
msgid "Reverb is commonly outputted from :ref:`Area3Ds <class_Area3D>` (see :ref:`Reverb buses <doc_audio_streams_reverb_buses>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:315
msgid "SpectrumAnalyzer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:317
msgid "Plots the amplitude of the audio signal within specified frequency ranges. This would typically be used for real-time audio visualization, like a spectrogram. Visualizing voices can be a great way to draw attention to them without increasing their volume. This effect does not alter audio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:324
msgid "Accessing :ref:`AudioEffectSpectrumAnalyzerInstance <class_AudioEffectSpectrumAnalyzerInstance>` is necessary to make use of this effect. A demo project using this can be found `here <https://github.com/godotengine/godot-demo-projects/tree/master/audio/spectrum>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:330
msgid "StereoEnhance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/audio/audio_effects.rst:332
msgid "Adjusts the gain of the left and right channels, and makes mono sounds stereo through phase shift. This can be used to widen or narrow a sound. Headphones are recommended when configuring this effect."
msgstr ""
